        "id": 128953,
        "name": "Silverleaf",
        "username": "Silverleaf",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/silverleaf/{size}/2332_2.png",
        "created_at": "2015-01-22T17:11:39.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>Actually I think I mean \"Old English\" as opposed to \"old English\".</p><p>It's not just a slang word for woman (although we still use it like that - \"Haven't seen Harry around for ages, I think he's got a new bird\"), the word \"bird\" originally <em>literally&nbsp;</em>meant \"young woman\", rather than \"feathery flying thing\". Smaller feathery flying things eventually got nicknamed \"birds\" for whatever reason, and then the word was generalised to encompass larger feathery flying things which were previously called \"fowl\".</p><p>The name \"blackbird\" (type of Old World thrush common in Europe) is interesting because today it doesn't sound terribly descriptive - there are several species in the UK which could be described as \"black birds\", like crows, rooks, ravens and jackdaws. Why did blackbirds get to be&nbsp;<em>the</em> black bird? Because it's the only black \"bird\" - the rest were \"fowl\".</p>",
        "post_number": 21,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T04:03:48.540Z",
        "reply_count": 0,
