      {
        "id": 128958,
        "name": "skoticus",
        "username": "skoticus",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/skoticus/{size}/1964_2.png",
        "created_at": "2015-01-22T20:41:00.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>I'm studyig Old English right now (part of a PhD in English) and I just did some snooping around. The Oxford English Dictionary doesn't support \"young woman\" being the original definition of \"bird\". Instead, \"bird\" meant what we might now call \"chick\", ie, a young bird. But there are examples of \"bird\" being any young animal or beast. Opposite of the claims above, the OED even shows it as meaning \"young man\" or \"son\" occationally (poetically).</p><p><span>However, the OED does show that around 1400 (the Middle English period) \"bird\" started to be used to mean \"young woman\", and suggests that this might be a textual error, confusing it with \"burd\" (a Middle English word meaning </span><em>bride</em><span>).&nbsp;</span></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p>",
        "post_number": 26,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T04:03:48.870Z",
        "reply_count": 0,
        "reply_to_post_number": null,
